1
00:01:21,949 --> 00:01:23,850
Retractor.

2
00:01:26,386 --> 00:01:27,979
Hemostat.

3
00:01:34,561 --> 00:01:36,189
Hemostat.

4
00:01:38,198 --> 00:01:39,996
Sorry, doctor.

5
00:01:40,334 --> 00:01:42,166
Since you obviously
need to practice,

6
00:01:42,336 --> 00:01:43,998
put it back and
we'll try it again.

7
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:46,402
- It wasn't my fault...
- Hemostat!

8
00:01:49,276 --> 00:01:52,405
I'm not interested in your excuses
or anything else you have to say.

9
00:01:52,579 --> 00:01:54,980
So from now on, you
keep your mouth shut.

10
00:01:57,651 --> 00:02:00,018
Scalpel. Let's go,
let's go, move it.

11
00:02:00,187 --> 00:02:02,213
I'm moving as quickly
as safety allows.

12
00:02:02,389 --> 00:02:04,255
All right, that's
it. Get out of here!

13
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:07,258
- You, take over, hurry up.
- Yes, doctor.

14
00:02:09,563 --> 00:02:11,225
Scalpel.

15
00:02:11,698 --> 00:02:13,166
Scalpel.

16
00:02:18,639 --> 00:02:20,767
What's going on?

17
00:02:21,341 --> 00:02:24,106
Gray just kicked
me out of the O.R.

18
00:02:24,411 --> 00:02:26,710
He was absolutely insufferable.

19
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,178
It's the divorce.

20
00:02:28,348 --> 00:02:31,807
His wife's trying to squeeze
every dime she can out of him.

21
00:02:31,985 --> 00:02:34,147
Well, bless her heart.

22
00:02:35,489 --> 00:02:37,515
Insubordinate and incompetent.

23
00:02:37,691 --> 00:02:39,182
I'm sick to death
of people like her.

24
00:02:39,359 --> 00:02:41,294
It's okay, Bradley. She's gone.

25
00:02:41,461 --> 00:02:44,192
She has no business
being anywhere near an O.R.

26
00:02:44,364 --> 00:02:45,525
Good Lord, suction.

27
00:02:45,699 --> 00:02:47,031
Suction.

28
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:49,931
- Hemostat.
- Hemostat.

29
00:02:50,904 --> 00:02:52,532
- Sponge.
- Sponge.

30
00:02:52,973 --> 00:02:56,102
My God, her kidney's bleeding
like there's no tomorrow.

31
00:02:56,276 --> 00:02:58,711
- Three units.
- I didn't go that deep, did I?

32
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:00,245
Looked to me
like you severed it.

33
00:03:18,365 --> 00:03:20,391
So, what happens now?

34
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:22,993
Now we wait till the
patient's out of recovery.

35
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:25,230
- And then you tell her?
- Of course I tell her.

36
00:03:25,405 --> 00:03:27,725
What did you think I was gonna
do? Let her walk out of here

37
00:03:27,874 --> 00:03:29,740
with a damaged kidney,
hope she never noticed?

38
00:03:29,910 --> 00:03:33,039
I'm sorry. I don't know
what the proper procedure is.

39
00:03:33,213 --> 00:03:34,647
This is a first for me.

40
00:03:35,749 --> 00:03:38,150
It's not exactly something
I do every day either.

41
00:03:38,318 --> 00:03:40,082
I wasn't suggesting that it was.

42
00:03:40,554 --> 00:03:42,921
Come on, Bradley, take it easy.

43
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:46,083
First thing we do
is tell the patient.

44
00:03:46,793 --> 00:03:49,160
We meaning you and I,
so that nobody can say

45
00:03:49,329 --> 00:03:51,821
that I misrepresented
the facts to her.

46
00:03:52,666 --> 00:03:55,465
I take it back. That's the
second thing that we do.

47
00:03:57,838 --> 00:03:59,830
- You gotta be kidding me.
- No.

48
00:04:00,006 --> 00:04:02,339
It took him ten minutes
to stop the bleeding.

49
00:04:02,509 --> 00:04:04,501
I mean, he just creamed it.

50
00:04:04,678 --> 00:04:09,810
For all the good it's gonna do her
now, he may as well have just removed it.

51
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:15,045
Nurse Stone, your behavior in
the O.R. today was inexcusable.

52
00:04:15,222 --> 00:04:18,158
By distracting me like that,
you cost a woman her kidney.

53
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:19,349
You're blaming me?

54
00:04:19,526 --> 00:04:22,860
And after I speak to the administrator,
it will have cost you your job.

55
00:04:29,536 --> 00:04:30,799
Oh...

56
00:04:32,806 --> 00:04:34,570
Hello, Nancy.

57
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:38,944
This is Dr. Conway. He
assisted me during your surgery.

58
00:04:39,112 --> 00:04:41,547
Actually, we met before,
just outside the O.R.

59
00:04:41,915 --> 00:04:44,275
I know you probably don't
remember. You were a little loopy.

60
00:04:44,417 --> 00:04:46,909
Oh, I was high as a kite. Heh.

61
00:04:48,255 --> 00:04:50,156
So how you doing?

62
00:04:50,323 --> 00:04:51,689
You tell me.

63
00:04:51,858 --> 00:04:53,986
Well, your back is
gonna be just fine.

64
00:04:54,161 --> 00:04:56,494
As I told you in your
pre-op appointment,

65
00:04:56,663 --> 00:04:57,687
for the next few months,

66
00:04:57,864 --> 00:05:00,129
you're gonna experience
some pain and lack of mobility,

67
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:03,270
but in time and
with proper exercise,

68
00:05:03,436 --> 00:05:05,337
your back is gonna
be as good as new.

69
00:05:05,505 --> 00:05:08,441
Unless, of course, you go
rupturing two more discs.

70
00:05:16,049 --> 00:05:18,518
So, what's wrong?

71
00:05:19,286 --> 00:05:20,754
Well...

72
00:05:22,189 --> 00:05:23,555
In the course of the surgery,

73
00:05:23,723 --> 00:05:27,387
I'm afraid that one of your
kidneys, the right one, was damaged.

74
00:05:27,961 --> 00:05:29,020
What do you mean?

75
00:05:29,196 --> 00:05:31,188
It was lacerated inadvertently.

76
00:05:31,364 --> 00:05:34,300
As I told you, the kidneys
are in very close proximity

77
00:05:34,467 --> 00:05:36,936
- to the surgical site...
- How bad is it?

78
00:05:37,103 --> 00:05:39,732
Well, I can't say
with certainty,

79
00:05:41,107 --> 00:05:44,100
but the kidney appears to be
rather severely compromised,

80
00:05:44,277 --> 00:05:46,746
probably to the point
of being nonfunctional.

81
00:05:49,115 --> 00:05:50,606
Oh...

82
00:05:50,917 --> 00:05:54,410
Oh, my God, you mean I'm gonna
have to spend the rest of my life

83
00:05:54,588 --> 00:05:56,708
hooked up to some kind of
dialysis machine or some...?

84
00:05:56,823 --> 00:05:59,725
No. No, no, no.

85
00:05:59,893 --> 00:06:02,124
- Nothing like that at all.
- No.

86
00:06:02,295 --> 00:06:05,094
In all likelihood, you'll experience
no ill effects whatsoever.

87
00:06:05,265 --> 00:06:07,734
That's right. Your left
kidney is more than capable

88
00:06:07,901 --> 00:06:09,733
of taking care of
things all by itself.

89
00:06:09,903 --> 00:06:12,998
People live long, happy, healthy
lives with only one kidney all the time.

90
00:06:13,173 --> 00:06:15,335
What if something
happens to my left kidney?

91
00:06:15,508 --> 00:06:18,103
Well, chances are nothing will.

92
00:06:23,683 --> 00:06:25,777
Who did it? You?

93
00:06:26,286 --> 00:06:27,310
No.

94
00:06:27,487 --> 00:06:30,047
No, I'm afraid I'm responsible.

95
00:06:31,625 --> 00:06:35,357
I was distracted at a
highly inopportune moment.

96
00:06:35,862 --> 00:06:37,023
I'm sorry.

97
00:06:40,267 --> 00:06:43,396
You destroy a perfectly
healthy part of my body,

98
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:47,234
and all you can
say is you're sorry?

99
00:06:47,407 --> 00:06:50,002
There are risks associated
with all surgeries,

100
00:06:50,176 --> 00:06:52,941
as I pointed out to you
when you signed your release.

101
00:06:53,113 --> 00:06:55,113
I signed that release
because you led me to believe

102
00:06:55,282 --> 00:06:57,547
that I was in the hands
of a highly skilled surgeon,

103
00:06:57,717 --> 00:07:00,653
not some absent-minded butcher.

104
00:07:01,054 --> 00:07:03,922
I'd be happy to recommend
a very good kidney specialist

105
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:06,025
- who can explain...
- No. No more doctors.

106
00:07:07,794 --> 00:07:11,492
The only thing I want right now is
the name of a really good lawyer.

107
00:08:26,706 --> 00:08:30,006
- Thanks for coming so soon...
- Hi.

108
00:08:30,176 --> 00:08:34,272
- Heh. I thought you were the roofer.
- Oh.

109
00:08:35,181 --> 00:08:37,673
Can I come in anyway?

110
00:08:41,788 --> 00:08:43,222
Mom gave me this address.

111
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:46,258
Told me this is where you
spend most of your time.

112
00:08:46,593 --> 00:08:48,994
Yeah, the man I
work for lives here,

113
00:08:49,162 --> 00:08:51,893
and, uh, he works out
of his house, so I do too.

114
00:08:52,065 --> 00:08:53,966
Oh...

115
00:08:54,567 --> 00:08:57,247
For a second there I thought maybe
you're shacking up with somebody.

116
00:08:59,005 --> 00:09:01,565
So, what are you doing here?
How come you're not at work?

117
00:09:03,643 --> 00:09:05,578
I lost my job.

118
00:09:05,745 --> 00:09:08,146
The hospital laid a bunch of
people off a couple months ago,

119
00:09:08,314 --> 00:09:09,441
and I was one of them.

120
00:09:11,217 --> 00:09:13,948
So I figured I had some spare
time, I'd come down for a visit.

121
00:09:14,988 --> 00:09:16,479
And we'd do what?

122
00:09:17,157 --> 00:09:20,286
Oh, I don't know.
Hang out, try to talk.

123
00:09:22,762 --> 00:09:24,754
It's been a long time.

124
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:29,896
You didn't drive across two
states to come and visit me.

125
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:33,097
You never visited me
when you lived across town.

126
00:09:33,907 --> 00:09:35,205
You didn't get laid off.

127
00:09:36,443 --> 00:09:38,674
You got fired, didn't you?

128
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:42,511
So, what is it, Rachel,
you, uh...? You broke?

129
00:09:42,682 --> 00:09:43,945
You need a handout?

130
00:09:44,117 --> 00:09:47,110
God, I don't believe
this. You... Oh...

131
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:49,252
You know what ticks me
off the most about you?

132
00:09:49,422 --> 00:09:54,326
It isn't the lying or your inability
to take responsibility for anything.

133
00:09:54,494 --> 00:09:57,794
It's that you think so little of me, you
still believe you can get away with this.

134
00:09:57,964 --> 00:09:59,626
Why do you always
think that I'm lying?

135
00:09:59,799 --> 00:10:01,700
Rachel, you don't know
how to tell the truth.

136
00:10:01,868 --> 00:10:04,770
- God, Jerri, that was a long time ago.
- You're always doctoring it up.

137
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:07,339
You're always twisting it
around to fit your little purposes.

138
00:10:07,507 --> 00:10:09,533
Okay, you know what?
Forget it, just forget it.

139
00:10:09,709 --> 00:10:12,406
I'm gonna go, and you are never
gonna hear from me again, all right?

140
00:10:12,579 --> 00:10:13,842
How is that?

141
00:10:14,013 --> 00:10:16,073
Trust me, I'll
never be that lucky!

142
00:10:18,118 --> 00:10:19,780
Who was that?

143
00:10:20,019 --> 00:10:22,420
- My sister.
- Oh.

144
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:13,868
Speed limit's the same here
as it is in North Carolina, ma'am.

145
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,134
My radar gun says
you were going 80.

146
00:11:16,309 --> 00:11:19,711
Oh, no way I was
going that fast.

147
00:11:19,879 --> 00:11:21,799
I mean, I didn't feel
like I was going that fast.

148
00:11:21,948 --> 00:11:24,508
License and
registration, please.

149
00:11:24,684 --> 00:11:26,152
Sure.

150
00:11:39,065 --> 00:11:42,695
Put your hands on your head
and very slowly get out of the car.

151
00:11:42,869 --> 00:11:44,303
What...?

152
00:11:44,604 --> 00:11:46,402
What's going on?

153
00:11:47,106 --> 00:11:49,302
There's an all-points
bulletin out on you.

154
00:11:49,475 --> 00:11:51,467
You're wanted in
Wilmington, North Carolina,

155
00:11:51,644 --> 00:11:52,668
for murder.

156
00:11:54,581 --> 00:12:00,282
Case 95 48625-4-3.

157
00:12:00,453 --> 00:12:03,753
<i>The People v. Rachel Stone.</i>

158
00:12:03,923 --> 00:12:05,255
What are the charges?

159
00:12:05,425 --> 00:12:08,156
The state charges that Rachel
Stone murdered Dr. Bradley Gray

160
00:12:08,328 --> 00:12:12,663
on March 7th, 1995, with
premeditation, Your Honor.

161
00:12:12,832 --> 00:12:15,358
Have you retained counsel?

162
00:12:15,735 --> 00:12:17,397
I can't afford a lawyer.

163
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:19,402
Well, then one will
be supplied for you.

164
00:12:19,572 --> 00:12:23,100
That won't be necessary,
Your Honor. I'll take it.

165
00:12:31,384 --> 00:12:34,354
- It looked like you were fleeing.
- I wasn't fleeing.

166
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:36,489
I didn't even know
Dr. Gray was dead.

167
00:12:36,656 --> 00:12:37,988
Why were you heading south?

168
00:12:38,157 --> 00:12:41,355
I was going to Valdosta.
Cousin Martha just moved there.

169
00:12:41,527 --> 00:12:43,928
And you were gonna hit
her up for some money.

170
00:12:44,097 --> 00:12:46,032
- I was not.
- Why else would you go see her?

171
00:12:46,199 --> 00:12:49,169
- You can't stand Cousin Martha.
- Oh, how the hell would you know?

172
00:12:49,335 --> 00:12:51,167
- I know.
- All right, all right, all right.

173
00:12:51,337 --> 00:12:53,329
Can we quit talking
about Cousin Martha?

174
00:12:53,506 --> 00:12:55,202
Let's talk about Dr. Gray.

175
00:12:55,375 --> 00:12:58,641
Dr. Gray was an orthopedic
surgeon at Shoreline General.

176
00:12:58,811 --> 00:13:00,177
It was the hospital
that I worked.

177
00:13:00,346 --> 00:13:04,181
He was a real egomaniac, I
mean, horrible to work with.

178
00:13:04,350 --> 00:13:05,875
Anyway, about four months ago,

179
00:13:06,052 --> 00:13:08,078
he accidentally damaged
a patient's kidney

180
00:13:08,254 --> 00:13:11,782
during an operation I
assisted on, and he blamed me,

181
00:13:11,958 --> 00:13:13,654
and he got me fired.

182
00:13:13,826 --> 00:13:16,557
And this was not your
fault in the slightest?

183
00:13:16,729 --> 00:13:17,958
That's right.

184
00:13:18,131 --> 00:13:20,532
He fired you for no
reason? Come on, Rachel.

185
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:22,168
Oh, the guy made a mistake.

186
00:13:22,335 --> 00:13:24,964
It cost his insurance
company 1.5 million bucks.

187
00:13:25,138 --> 00:13:26,778
He had to blame
somebody, so he blamed me.

188
00:13:26,906 --> 00:13:29,637
- Right, right.
- All right, all right, all right.

189
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:32,404
What about the scalpel the
police found in your house?

190
00:13:32,578 --> 00:13:34,604
I have no idea
where that came from.

191
00:13:34,781 --> 00:13:38,775
Somebody must've put it there after I
left for Atlanta, that's all I can figure.

192
00:13:38,951 --> 00:13:40,715
Three people say

193
00:13:40,887 --> 00:13:44,324
they saw you at the hospital
the day of the murder.

194
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:46,083
Did they?

195
00:13:46,259 --> 00:13:48,785
Yeah, I've been looking
and looking for another job,

196
00:13:48,961 --> 00:13:50,759
and no one would hire me

197
00:13:50,930 --> 00:13:53,957
because Dr. Gray badmouthed me
to hospital administrators all over town.

198
00:13:54,133 --> 00:13:55,362
So I went to see him.

199
00:13:55,535 --> 00:13:57,436
I expected him to
give me a break.

200
00:13:57,603 --> 00:13:59,003
And he what...?

201
00:13:59,172 --> 00:14:01,539
Heh. Not a chance, no. Heh.

202
00:14:01,708 --> 00:14:04,439
So I packed up my
car, I headed for Atlanta.

203
00:14:04,610 --> 00:14:06,579
Figured a little help
from my only sister,

204
00:14:06,746 --> 00:14:08,908
maybe I could start
over down there.

205
00:14:09,082 --> 00:14:10,243
Wrong.

206
00:14:10,416 --> 00:14:13,818
And this was the last time you
saw Dr. Gray since you were let go?

207
00:14:13,986 --> 00:14:15,454
Yeah.

208
00:14:16,556 --> 00:14:18,156
I should never have
gotten you into this.

209
00:14:18,324 --> 00:14:20,259
- I am so sorry.
- What? What?

210
00:14:20,426 --> 00:14:22,327
You had a loud
argument with Dr. Gray

211
00:14:22,495 --> 00:14:25,158
outside the Parkway Grill
ten days before the murder.

212
00:14:25,331 --> 00:14:27,960
- At least four people saw you.
- I forgot, I am sorry.

213
00:14:28,134 --> 00:14:30,660
Rachel, we have gotta
get something straight here.

214
00:14:30,837 --> 00:14:33,739
Mr. Matlock is one of the
best lawyers in this country.

215
00:14:33,906 --> 00:14:35,568
He is doing this
as a favor to me,

216
00:14:35,742 --> 00:14:37,902
but he cannot help you if
you don't tell him the truth.

217
00:14:38,044 --> 00:14:40,104
We just happened to run
into each other, that's all.

218
00:14:40,279 --> 00:14:42,759
He said something snotty to
me, I said something snotty to him.

219
00:14:42,915 --> 00:14:45,835
That's all it was, it was nothing.
That's all. That's all it was, nothing.

220
00:14:45,985 --> 00:14:48,386
Then you go see if you can
give us a look at the crime scene,

221
00:14:48,554 --> 00:14:49,754
I'll take care of your sister.

222
00:14:49,922 --> 00:14:51,802
- You better keep a polygraph handy.
- Go ahead.

223
00:14:51,958 --> 00:14:55,395
- Oh, that's funny. That's really cute.
- Go ahead, go ahead. Okay, let's go.

224
00:14:59,899 --> 00:15:01,094
He was only stabbed once?

225
00:15:01,267 --> 00:15:03,463
One time, right
in that main artery.

226
00:15:03,636 --> 00:15:06,868
Either the killer got lucky
or he knew his anatomy.

227
00:15:07,039 --> 00:15:09,634
- Any fingerprints on the scalpel?
- No, latex.

228
00:15:09,809 --> 00:15:12,574
Probably wore surgical gloves.

229
00:15:12,912 --> 00:15:15,746
And since Rachel's a surgical
nurse, to say nothing of the fact

230
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:19,079
that the murder weapon
was found in her living room...

231
00:15:19,752 --> 00:15:21,983
The prosecutor's gonna
have a field day, isn't he?

232
00:15:22,155 --> 00:15:25,887
Well, I know I would.

233
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:30,123
My sister has her faults.

234
00:15:30,863 --> 00:15:34,493
Well, all of us do.

235
00:15:36,402 --> 00:15:38,337
But she'd never kill anybody.

236
00:15:38,504 --> 00:15:40,097
It's not in her nature.

237
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:42,868
When we were kids...

238
00:15:43,276 --> 00:15:45,472
- Hey, guys.
- Hi.

239
00:15:45,778 --> 00:15:47,679
- Have I got some interesting news.
- What?

240
00:15:47,847 --> 00:15:50,339
Phone records. Guess
who Dr. Bradley Gray called

241
00:15:50,516 --> 00:15:52,116
every day, at least
once a day, for weeks

242
00:15:52,285 --> 00:15:54,311
- up until the day he was murdered.
- Who?

243
00:15:54,487 --> 00:15:55,785
Nancy Pierson.

244
00:15:55,955 --> 00:15:57,235
The girl whose kidney he damaged

245
00:15:57,390 --> 00:15:59,222
during that operation
that got Rachel fired.

246
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:03,329
Wonder why.

247
00:16:37,864 --> 00:16:39,457
- Aah!
- Oh.

248
00:16:39,665 --> 00:16:40,997
- I'm sorry.
- Oh.

249
00:16:41,167 --> 00:16:43,227
Oh, I'm sorry.

250
00:16:43,402 --> 00:16:48,200
I'm sorry. Ah. Oh...
Are you...? Excuse me.

251
00:16:48,374 --> 00:16:50,275
Are you Nancy Pierson?

252
00:16:50,443 --> 00:16:52,878
- Yes, sir.
- Oh, I'm, uh...

253
00:16:53,045 --> 00:16:54,069
I'm Ben Matlock.

254
00:16:54,247 --> 00:16:58,014
I saw you weren't here, and I
just thought I'd sit down and wait.

255
00:16:58,184 --> 00:16:59,846
And I guess I dozed off.

256
00:17:00,019 --> 00:17:02,284
- Did I drool?
- Heh.

257
00:17:02,455 --> 00:17:05,220
- No, you just snored.
- Oh, well...

258
00:17:05,725 --> 00:17:09,958
Uh, anyway, I'm a lawyer,
and I'm representing the woman

259
00:17:10,129 --> 00:17:12,621
who's accused of
murdering Bradley Gray.

260
00:17:12,798 --> 00:17:15,768
And I wondered if we
could, uh, talk a few minutes.

261
00:17:15,935 --> 00:17:17,904
Oh, certainly, I have
all the time in the world.

262
00:17:18,070 --> 00:17:19,732
Oh. Ah.

263
00:17:22,141 --> 00:17:24,110
- Oh.
- Uh-oh. Your back still bothering you?

264
00:17:24,277 --> 00:17:26,872
Oh, not as bad as
it was, believe me.

265
00:17:27,046 --> 00:17:29,709
Just knowing that I'll never have
to pick up another suitcase again

266
00:17:29,882 --> 00:17:31,714
- helps immensely.
- Oh, that's right.

267
00:17:31,884 --> 00:17:34,752
You used to work as a
ticket agent, didn't you?

268
00:17:34,921 --> 00:17:38,653
Twelve years, throwing
luggage onto a conveyer belt.

269
00:17:38,824 --> 00:17:41,692
One day I grabbed ahold of
an innocent-looking duffle bag,

270
00:17:41,861 --> 00:17:44,490
- pulled and ruptured two discs.
- Ooh.

271
00:17:44,964 --> 00:17:46,091
Tell me about it.

272
00:17:46,599 --> 00:17:49,569
But Dr. Gray made it all better.

273
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:51,226
I lost a kidney in the process,

274
00:17:51,404 --> 00:17:54,067
but I also gained more
than a million dollars,

275
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:56,232
- Ooh.
- So I'd say it all worked out.

276
00:17:57,610 --> 00:18:02,139
Uh, did you talk to Dr. Gray
after you decided to sue?

277
00:18:02,315 --> 00:18:06,150
That man... Must have called
me every day for a month.

278
00:18:06,319 --> 00:18:09,414
- Why?
- Trying to get me to drop the lawsuit.

279
00:18:09,589 --> 00:18:12,718
He kept ranting and raving about
how it was gonna ruin his reputation,

280
00:18:12,892 --> 00:18:13,916
like I cared.

281
00:18:15,795 --> 00:18:18,424
When did the insurance
company settle with you?

282
00:18:19,165 --> 00:18:23,468
Um... I deposited
the check on the 6th.

283
00:18:25,204 --> 00:18:28,538
That would've been the
day before he was murdered.

284
00:18:29,875 --> 00:18:31,343
Does that mean something?

285
00:18:33,045 --> 00:18:34,673
Probably not.

286
00:18:34,847 --> 00:18:36,440
Probably not.

287
00:18:38,284 --> 00:18:40,014
We can talk in here.

288
00:18:40,186 --> 00:18:42,746
As long as it doesn't take too
long. I need to go on rounds.

289
00:18:42,922 --> 00:18:46,825
- Oh, you're a resident?
- Third-year surgical resident, yes.

290
00:18:47,627 --> 00:18:51,029
Oh, you got one of those
blood pressure gizmos in here.

291
00:18:51,197 --> 00:18:53,428
Sphygmomanometer.

292
00:18:53,666 --> 00:18:56,636
The technical name for
it, sphygmomanometer.

293
00:18:57,937 --> 00:18:59,997
You know, the last
time I saw my doctor,

294
00:19:00,172 --> 00:19:02,732
he said my pressure
was a little high.

295
00:19:02,908 --> 00:19:06,606
- You were probably just nervous.
- Oh, I was. But I'm not now.

296
00:19:06,779 --> 00:19:10,045
I'm here and you're here
and it's here, everything's here,

297
00:19:10,216 --> 00:19:11,411
so how about it?

298
00:19:11,584 --> 00:19:13,944
Mr. Matlock, we shouldn't
even be talking while I'm on duty.

299
00:19:14,086 --> 00:19:16,351
Well, you can
take it while we talk.

300
00:19:16,522 --> 00:19:19,390
It'll just take a second,
and I won't tell anybody.

301
00:19:22,762 --> 00:19:26,392
Ah. I understand you're
the one who found the body.

302
00:19:26,565 --> 00:19:28,329
Yes. It was quite a shock.

303
00:19:28,501 --> 00:19:30,129
I imagine.

304
00:19:30,302 --> 00:19:32,931
Well, what were you doing
at the hospital on your day off?

305
00:19:33,105 --> 00:19:34,985
Well, I needed to get
some stuff from my locker.

306
00:19:35,141 --> 00:19:38,061
I stopped in to see Bradley. We'd
had a couple of run-ins during the week,

307
00:19:38,210 --> 00:19:40,441
- and I just wanted to clear the air.
- Run-ins?

308
00:19:41,313 --> 00:19:43,077
Oh, just silly little arguments

309
00:19:43,249 --> 00:19:46,083
about things that were
totally inconsequential.

310
00:19:46,385 --> 00:19:49,184
And then this malpractice
case was making him crazy.

311
00:19:49,355 --> 00:19:51,347
I mean, coming on top
of his divorce like that.

312
00:19:51,524 --> 00:19:54,289
He just made sure that everyone
was just as miserable as he was.

313
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:58,192
Was he driving
his ex-wife crazy?

314
00:19:58,364 --> 00:20:00,765
Oh, no, she was happy as a
clam. She got half his money

315
00:20:00,933 --> 00:20:04,062
and made a beeline
for the Greek isles. Eh.

316
00:20:04,236 --> 00:20:06,716
- Oh, what's the matter?
- I just couldn't hear the first time.

317
00:20:06,872 --> 00:20:09,307
- We were talking.
- Oh.

318
00:20:10,443 --> 00:20:14,539
Uh, I understand that
you assisted Dr. Gray

319
00:20:14,714 --> 00:20:17,309
in the operation that
caused the malpractice suit.

320
00:20:17,483 --> 00:20:18,917
That's right.

321
00:20:19,251 --> 00:20:22,744
Was Rachel Stone to
blame for what happened?

322
00:20:23,589 --> 00:20:27,424
Bradley allowed himself to be
distracted at a critical moment

323
00:20:27,593 --> 00:20:29,289
and made a critical error.

324
00:20:29,462 --> 00:20:30,930
No one was to blame but him.

325
00:20:35,134 --> 00:20:37,763
- What? What?
- I'm done.

326
00:20:37,937 --> 00:20:39,030
It's still high, isn't it?

327
00:20:39,205 --> 00:20:41,445
Mr. Matlock, if you're worried
about your blood pressure,

328
00:20:41,607 --> 00:20:43,087
I suggest you see
your regular doctor.

329
00:20:43,242 --> 00:20:45,734
Now, if you'll excuse me,
I need to go on rounds.

330
00:20:47,113 --> 00:20:49,981
Well, you could give me a pill.

331
00:21:08,701 --> 00:21:11,637
I guess you know who
came to see me today.

332
00:21:12,705 --> 00:21:14,697
We need to talk.

333
00:21:27,086 --> 00:21:28,748
Hello, doctor.

334
00:22:26,545 --> 00:22:28,411
Dr. Conway.

335
00:22:31,984 --> 00:22:34,647
What are you doing there?
That room is off-limits.

336
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:38,452
Well, uh, I know,
but I... I just...

337
00:22:38,624 --> 00:22:40,855
Maybe you better give me that.

338
00:22:42,428 --> 00:22:43,691
<i>Why didn't you tell us?</i>

339
00:22:45,531 --> 00:22:47,329
Tell you what?

340
00:22:47,499 --> 00:22:50,094
About the file that
was in Dr. Gray's office.

341
00:22:50,269 --> 00:22:51,760
The one containing a memo

342
00:22:51,937 --> 00:22:54,406
describing how he caught
you stealing painkillers

343
00:22:54,573 --> 00:22:57,065
from the hospital dispensary.

344
00:22:58,143 --> 00:23:00,305
The one he made you sign.

345
00:23:00,479 --> 00:23:02,607
I made a copy of it.

346
00:23:05,885 --> 00:23:07,683
The Trioxin wasn't for me.

347
00:23:07,853 --> 00:23:09,822
It was for my boyfriend.

348
00:23:09,989 --> 00:23:11,924
And I wasn't stealing.
I was gonna replace it.

349
00:23:12,091 --> 00:23:14,686
- Oh, for heaven's sake.
- I wasn't stealing it, Jerri.

350
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:18,763
Dr. Gray believed me. That's
why he never reported it.

351
00:23:20,266 --> 00:23:23,532
That memo was just his way of
making sure I'd never do it again.

352
00:23:23,702 --> 00:23:26,729
That memo is going to
allow the prosecutor to say

353
00:23:26,906 --> 00:23:28,534
that you killed him
not for revenge,

354
00:23:28,707 --> 00:23:31,905
but so that nobody would
find out what you did.

355
00:23:32,077 --> 00:23:35,445
Can't you see how that
makes everything worse?

356
00:23:44,723 --> 00:23:47,818
I knew if anyone found
that memo it'd be a disaster.

357
00:23:49,895 --> 00:23:51,830
You should've told me.

358
00:23:58,404 --> 00:24:02,068
Did you ask Dr. Conway
to steal it for you?

359
00:24:02,441 --> 00:24:03,932
He volunteered.

360
00:24:04,343 --> 00:24:06,107
He's a really good friend.

361
00:24:08,547 --> 00:24:09,947
I...

362
00:24:14,620 --> 00:24:17,556
He did it because I
stole the drugs for him.

363
00:24:40,779 --> 00:24:42,941
- Hello, Miss Pierson.
- Hello.

364
00:24:44,016 --> 00:24:47,009
Let's see now. Here you
go. Saw you sitting out here.

365
00:24:47,186 --> 00:24:50,884
- Thought I'd save you a trip to the box.
- Oh, well, that was nice. Thank you.

366
00:24:51,056 --> 00:24:53,821
I guess you're a woman
of leisure now, huh?

367
00:24:54,393 --> 00:24:56,487
- You betcha.
- It must be nice.

368
00:24:56,662 --> 00:24:59,222
- How's the back?
- It's getting better every day.

369
00:24:59,398 --> 00:25:01,333
Well, wish I could say
the same about mine.

370
00:25:01,500 --> 00:25:03,901
- Heh. Y'all take care.
- Ha-ha.

371
00:25:12,911 --> 00:25:14,641
Help!

372
00:25:14,813 --> 00:25:16,509
I can't breathe.

373
00:25:20,252 --> 00:25:21,845
Help! Please!

374
00:25:29,995 --> 00:25:33,295
It was my first year of
residency. I was a basket case.

375
00:25:33,465 --> 00:25:36,697
And I wasn't the only one. I mean,
everybody I knew was popping pills.

376
00:25:36,869 --> 00:25:38,963
There's a big difference
between popping pills

377
00:25:39,138 --> 00:25:40,162
and stealing them.

378
00:25:40,339 --> 00:25:41,739
I was gonna have
Rachel replace them

379
00:25:41,874 --> 00:25:43,775
as soon as I got the
prescription refilled.

380
00:25:43,942 --> 00:25:46,703
And if Bradley Gray hadn't come
along, nobody would've been the wiser.

381
00:25:46,845 --> 00:25:52,079
Oh. Did, uh, he know that you were
the one she was stealing them for?

382
00:25:52,251 --> 00:25:55,221
Not stealing, borrowing.

383
00:25:55,387 --> 00:25:57,219
And no, she never told him.

384
00:25:57,389 --> 00:26:00,359
So I had no reason to kill
him, if that's what this is about.

385
00:26:00,526 --> 00:26:02,757
It was in the back of my mind.

386
00:26:02,928 --> 00:26:05,557
- Now, you and Rachel are, uh...?
- Friends.

387
00:26:05,731 --> 00:26:07,097
Friends.

388
00:26:07,266 --> 00:26:09,064
She's a good person.

389
00:26:09,234 --> 00:26:11,100
I was furious when
Bradley Gray had her fired,

390
00:26:11,270 --> 00:26:13,262
but there was
nothing I could do.

391
00:26:13,806 --> 00:26:15,240
- Tell you one thing though.
- Mm.

392
00:26:15,407 --> 00:26:18,275
When I had to testify against
him in that malpractice case,

393
00:26:18,444 --> 00:26:19,776
I enjoyed the hell out of it.

394
00:26:21,914 --> 00:26:23,007
Oh, by the way,

395
00:26:23,182 --> 00:26:25,208
I made an appointment
with my regular doctor.

396
00:26:25,384 --> 00:26:27,584
- You know, about my blood pressure.
- Oh, good for you.

397
00:26:27,719 --> 00:26:29,519
I made an appointment.
It was high, wasn't it?

398
00:26:29,655 --> 00:26:30,679
Now, Mr. Matlock...

399
00:26:30,856 --> 00:26:33,382
You know, sometimes I
get this throbbing headache

400
00:26:33,559 --> 00:26:35,084
when I stand up real fast.

401
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:37,229
That's high blood
pressure, isn't it?

402
00:26:37,396 --> 00:26:39,422
- Give me the vitals.
- Blood pressure 90/40,

403
00:26:39,598 --> 00:26:41,078
pulse irregular,
airways constricted.

404
00:26:41,233 --> 00:26:43,073
Was fine one minute,
next she couldn't breathe.

405
00:26:43,235 --> 00:26:45,864
- Possible anaphylactic shock.
- Ten cc's of epinephrine.

406
00:26:46,038 --> 00:26:47,836
Do blood gases,
evacuate her stomach.

407
00:26:48,006 --> 00:26:50,339
Clear the hall! Keep
it down, move it!

408
00:26:50,509 --> 00:26:52,410
Mrs. Pierson.

409
00:26:57,916 --> 00:27:00,715
This is police
detective Hal Remsen.

410
00:27:00,886 --> 00:27:03,446
Mr. Matlock here thought
it would be a good idea

411
00:27:03,622 --> 00:27:05,955
if you told him
what you just told us.

412
00:27:06,125 --> 00:27:07,650
Now, do you feel up to it?

413
00:27:07,826 --> 00:27:09,886
Um, yeah, I guess.

414
00:27:10,996 --> 00:27:12,965
She's a very lucky woman.

415
00:27:13,132 --> 00:27:14,395
Hello, Miss Pierson.

416
00:27:14,566 --> 00:27:16,091
You wanna tell me what happened?

417
00:27:17,436 --> 00:27:20,736
Well, you see, I'm violently
allergic to benzocaine.

418
00:27:20,906 --> 00:27:22,966
That's a painkiller,
prescription only.

419
00:27:23,509 --> 00:27:26,673
Anyway, I took a garlic
pill right after lunch,

420
00:27:26,845 --> 00:27:28,575
like I always do,

421
00:27:28,747 --> 00:27:30,613
and next thing I
knew I was here.

422
00:27:30,782 --> 00:27:32,011
We pumped her stomach.

423
00:27:32,184 --> 00:27:35,279
We found remnants of Chinese
food and the garlic capsule,

424
00:27:35,454 --> 00:27:37,446
which appears to have been laced

425
00:27:37,623 --> 00:27:40,092
with a potentially fatal
dose of benzocaine.

426
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:43,641
How do you know the benzocaine
wasn't already in the food?

427
00:27:43,795 --> 00:27:45,635
Well, she would've gone
into anaphylactic shock

428
00:27:45,797 --> 00:27:47,288
right after the first bite.

429
00:27:47,466 --> 00:27:48,729
Probably be dead now.

430
00:27:49,368 --> 00:27:53,271
It was leftover moo goo gai
pan from the Lotus Garden.

431
00:27:53,438 --> 00:27:54,997
They wouldn't poison me.

432
00:27:55,174 --> 00:27:56,642
I'm too good a tipper.

433
00:27:57,276 --> 00:27:59,609
Where did you get
the garlic capsules?

434
00:27:59,778 --> 00:28:01,576
Mother Earth health food store.

435
00:28:01,747 --> 00:28:04,182
I keep a bottle of
them in my purse.

436
00:28:04,349 --> 00:28:06,784
It's supposed to prevent cancer.

437
00:28:07,786 --> 00:28:09,948
How many people know
you're allergic to benzocaine?

438
00:28:11,156 --> 00:28:12,249
A few.

439
00:28:12,624 --> 00:28:15,253
Any of them particularly
mad at you for any reason?

440
00:28:16,595 --> 00:28:18,564
Not that I know of.

441
00:28:18,730 --> 00:28:21,791
Dr. Gray was pretty
mad. Very mad.

442
00:28:22,334 --> 00:28:24,667
All those phone calls.

443
00:28:24,836 --> 00:28:27,772
That's the doctor she
sued for malpractice.

444
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:29,704
Dr. Bradley Gray?

445
00:28:29,875 --> 00:28:33,107
The man your client is
accused of having murdered?

446
00:28:33,278 --> 00:28:34,302
Yeah.

447
00:28:34,479 --> 00:28:36,038
So, what you're suggesting then

448
00:28:36,215 --> 00:28:39,617
is that he put the benzocaine in
the capsules before he was killed?

449
00:28:40,919 --> 00:28:42,387
Yeah, yeah.

450
00:28:42,554 --> 00:28:45,490
Yeah, you could say
that's what I'm saying.

451
00:28:49,561 --> 00:28:51,723
Hi. So, what's for dinner?

452
00:28:51,897 --> 00:28:54,332
- Stir-fry.
- Mm.

453
00:28:54,499 --> 00:28:57,901
- Any chance we can add a little...?
- No, Jerri, no meat.

454
00:28:58,070 --> 00:28:59,094
Oh, come on.

455
00:28:59,271 --> 00:29:02,002
One chicken breast
is not gonna kill you.

456
00:29:02,174 --> 00:29:05,167
Jerri, I do not want the flesh of
dead animals in my refrigerator.

457
00:29:05,711 --> 00:29:08,374
Jeez, Rachel, I'm your guest.

458
00:29:08,547 --> 00:29:10,743
Guest? You're my sister.

459
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:17,412
Explain something to me.

460
00:29:17,589 --> 00:29:20,616
We are, we're sisters,
yet we fight all the time.

461
00:29:20,792 --> 00:29:23,091
I mean, what happened to us?
We used to get along so well.

462
00:29:23,262 --> 00:29:24,355
We did?

463
00:29:24,529 --> 00:29:26,729
Yeah, remember when Mom
and Dad used to fight sometimes,

464
00:29:26,865 --> 00:29:29,130
we used to run upstairs and
we used to hide under my bed?

465
00:29:29,301 --> 00:29:33,762
No. You'd hide under your
bed, I'd hide under mine.

466
00:29:33,939 --> 00:29:36,374
Well, that's not
how I remember it.

467
00:29:36,541 --> 00:29:38,203
Rachel, you and I fought

468
00:29:38,377 --> 00:29:40,437
every waking moment
of our childhood.

469
00:29:40,612 --> 00:29:43,878
If we'd hid under a bed together,
one of us would've ended up dead

470
00:29:44,049 --> 00:29:45,347
within about two minutes.

471
00:29:45,517 --> 00:29:47,452
See? Why is that?

472
00:29:49,288 --> 00:29:50,881
I don't know.

473
00:29:51,123 --> 00:29:53,456
You and I are so different.

474
00:29:54,326 --> 00:29:57,296
I see things one way, you
see things another way.

475
00:29:57,763 --> 00:29:59,959
Only you have no
respect for my opinion,

476
00:30:00,132 --> 00:30:02,363
and I don't care for yours.

477
00:30:03,035 --> 00:30:04,401
It's always been that way.

478
00:30:05,170 --> 00:30:07,730
So I guess we just don't
like each other, do we?

479
00:30:09,007 --> 00:30:10,703
Hello?

480
00:30:10,942 --> 00:30:12,740
In the kitchen.

481
00:30:14,146 --> 00:30:19,449
Well, they're going to keep
Nancy Pierson under observation.

482
00:30:19,618 --> 00:30:21,246
That's in case she
goes into shock again.

483
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:23,150
- Mm-hm.
- Happens sometimes.

484
00:30:23,322 --> 00:30:25,291
Any idea who slipped
her the benzocaine?

485
00:30:26,091 --> 00:30:27,457
I think it was Dr. Gray.

486
00:30:28,293 --> 00:30:31,092
- Ben, that's crazy.
- No, it's not.

487
00:30:32,297 --> 00:30:35,631
I mean, if she was allergic to penicillin
and wore a medical alert bracelet,

488
00:30:35,801 --> 00:30:37,681
that's something anybody
who met her would know.

489
00:30:37,836 --> 00:30:39,771
But to be allergic
to benzocaine?

490
00:30:39,938 --> 00:30:42,066
That's something very few
people would know about.

491
00:30:42,240 --> 00:30:43,435
Her doctor would.

492
00:30:43,608 --> 00:30:45,839
Especially if he was
treating her for back pain.

493
00:30:47,579 --> 00:30:49,571
Wait a minute.
Are you suggesting

494
00:30:49,748 --> 00:30:53,344
that a renowned orthopedic
surgeon broke into her house

495
00:30:53,518 --> 00:30:55,316
to tamper with her garlic pills?

496
00:30:55,487 --> 00:30:58,719
If he wanted to kill her,
why go to such lengths?

497
00:30:58,890 --> 00:31:00,916
Well, you're probably right.

498
00:31:04,796 --> 00:31:06,924
So when are you gonna
go check out his house?

499
00:31:07,299 --> 00:31:10,428
Uh, Cliff's, uh, working
out the arrangements now.

500
00:31:10,602 --> 00:31:12,298
I'm gonna meet him
in about 30 minutes.

501
00:31:12,471 --> 00:31:16,875
Of course I could be a little
late if dinner's about ready.

502
00:31:17,142 --> 00:31:19,202
It's stir-fry. You're
welcome to stay.

503
00:31:19,745 --> 00:31:21,646
She puts tofu in it, Ben.

504
00:31:21,813 --> 00:31:26,342
No beef, no chicken, no fish.

505
00:31:26,518 --> 00:31:31,320
Just vegetables and white gunk.

506
00:31:32,357 --> 00:31:36,317
Oh, well, I probably
shouldn't keep Cliff waiting.

507
00:31:44,102 --> 00:31:46,037
He moved in after the divorce.

508
00:31:46,204 --> 00:31:47,331
It's nice enough.

509
00:31:47,506 --> 00:31:49,907
Oh, you should've seen
the house he lived in before.

510
00:31:50,075 --> 00:31:54,103
Right on the water, gorgeous view,
built-in vacuum-cleaning system.

511
00:31:54,279 --> 00:31:55,645
Mm-mm-mm.

512
00:31:55,814 --> 00:31:59,376
Mrs. Gray made him sell it so
she could get her half of the money.

513
00:31:59,551 --> 00:32:02,248
Ah. Well, it's mighty clean.
You do awfully good work.

514
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:05,516
Oh, thank you. But to be
honest, he never got it very dirty.

515
00:32:05,690 --> 00:32:07,454
I don't think he spent
that much time here.

516
00:32:07,626 --> 00:32:08,866
Well, how often do you come in?

517
00:32:08,994 --> 00:32:11,190
Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays.

518
00:32:12,431 --> 00:32:14,229
Mm...

519
00:32:15,667 --> 00:32:17,101
Hm.

520
00:32:20,539 --> 00:32:23,270
This bed doesn't look
like it's been slept in.

521
00:32:23,442 --> 00:32:25,638
- When did you change the sheets?
- Last Monday.

522
00:32:25,811 --> 00:32:27,507
I always did the
linens on Monday.

523
00:32:29,114 --> 00:32:31,709
He was killed the next day.

524
00:32:32,317 --> 00:32:35,082
Must have spent the
night somewheres else.

525
00:32:35,253 --> 00:32:37,916
- Maybe he stayed at the hospital.
- Yeah.

526
00:32:41,927 --> 00:32:44,294
Did Dr. Gray have a girlfriend?

527
00:32:44,463 --> 00:32:45,658
I'm sure he dated.

528
00:32:45,831 --> 00:32:47,595
Sometimes I could
smell a little perfume.

529
00:32:47,766 --> 00:32:51,294
- Ah. But you never saw her?
- Never.

530
00:32:52,137 --> 00:32:56,131
Heh. Sure didn't spend a lot
of time cooking, that's for sure.

531
00:32:57,843 --> 00:32:59,334
Aha.

532
00:32:59,778 --> 00:33:02,145
I guess he ate a lot
of takeout. Look at this.

533
00:33:02,314 --> 00:33:05,876
Willard's Rib House, Lotus
Garden, Wilmington Grill...

534
00:33:06,051 --> 00:33:09,146
- What was that Chinese?
- Lotus Garden.

535
00:33:16,995 --> 00:33:18,327
Hi.

536
00:33:18,497 --> 00:33:19,965
Can I come in?

537
00:33:20,665 --> 00:33:21,792
Sure.

538
00:33:22,167 --> 00:33:23,635
Thanks.

539
00:33:26,171 --> 00:33:29,335
- How are you doing?
- I feel fine.

540
00:33:29,508 --> 00:33:31,636
I don't know why
they're keeping me here.

541
00:33:31,810 --> 00:33:37,841
Well, the reason I stopped by is I
was at Bradley Gray's apartment,

542
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:41,282
and, uh, I think
he had a girlfriend.

543
00:33:42,320 --> 00:33:44,152
- Good for him.
- And I found out

544
00:33:44,322 --> 00:33:46,917
that he likes Chinese food.

545
00:33:47,092 --> 00:33:51,689
He ordered out from,
uh, Lotus Garden a lot.

546
00:33:53,465 --> 00:33:55,263
What a coincidence.

547
00:33:55,800 --> 00:33:57,098
I don't think so.

548
00:34:00,472 --> 00:34:03,101
And just what do you think?

549
00:34:03,675 --> 00:34:06,577
That girlfriend I mentioned,

550
00:34:07,279 --> 00:34:09,043
I think that was you.

551
00:34:18,256 --> 00:34:19,747
Oh, you can't be serious.

552
00:34:19,925 --> 00:34:21,951
Oh, yeah, yeah.

553
00:34:22,127 --> 00:34:25,461
That's how he knew
you take garlic pills.

554
00:34:25,630 --> 00:34:28,225
That's how he could
get to your purse so easy

555
00:34:28,400 --> 00:34:30,232
to lace one of them
with benzocaine.

556
00:34:30,769 --> 00:34:32,203
Explains the phone calls.

557
00:34:32,370 --> 00:34:34,839
He wasn't mad at
you. Quite the opposite.

558
00:34:35,407 --> 00:34:39,845
The two of you were in bed together,
in more ways than one, weren't you?

559
00:34:40,512 --> 00:34:43,380
You are out of your mind.

560
00:34:43,548 --> 00:34:45,414
Sooner or later,

561
00:34:45,617 --> 00:34:48,587
somebody's gonna remember
seeing the two of you together.

562
00:34:48,753 --> 00:34:50,483
And the whole
thing's gonna crumble.

563
00:34:50,655 --> 00:34:52,886
If I were you, I'd
let it go right now.

564
00:34:56,595 --> 00:34:59,064
I'm sorry, but you're
really starting to annoy me.

565
00:34:59,230 --> 00:35:02,598
Of course, I can, uh,
stop by your townhouse

566
00:35:02,767 --> 00:35:04,599
and see what I find there.

567
00:35:05,070 --> 00:35:07,369
You go near my place,
I'll have you arrested.

568
00:35:08,173 --> 00:35:09,368
<i>Yes?</i>

569
00:35:09,774 --> 00:35:13,802
Would you please come down here?
There's a man in here bothering me.

570
00:35:14,079 --> 00:35:16,207
Of course, I can
always get a warrant.

571
00:35:16,548 --> 00:35:18,244
You can go to
hell. Now, get out.

572
00:35:18,416 --> 00:35:19,907
Okay.

573
00:35:42,807 --> 00:35:45,333
So the scalpel was just
lying here in plain sight?

574
00:35:45,510 --> 00:35:48,105
Police found it on the floor
by the corner of that chair.

575
00:35:48,279 --> 00:35:49,941
Oh, does it really
make sense to them

576
00:35:50,115 --> 00:35:52,627
that I would kill somebody, then
just leave a bloody murder weapon

577
00:35:52,651 --> 00:35:55,120
- lying around my living room?
- They don't care.

578
00:35:55,286 --> 00:35:57,721
It's our job to try
to make sense of it.

579
00:35:58,456 --> 00:36:00,220
Did you lock all your
doors when you left?

580
00:36:00,392 --> 00:36:02,122
- Always.
- Windows?

581
00:36:02,293 --> 00:36:05,195
When I left town that day,
everything was locked and closed.

582
00:36:05,363 --> 00:36:08,629
- Hm.
- You must've left something open.

583
00:36:08,800 --> 00:36:11,497
How else could Nancy Pierson
have planted the scalpel in here?

584
00:36:11,670 --> 00:36:13,969
I mean, it's not like she's
a professional burglar.

585
00:36:17,909 --> 00:36:19,002
What about that?

586
00:36:19,177 --> 00:36:22,375
Oh, yeah, that's the only
thing that wasn't locked.

587
00:36:22,547 --> 00:36:24,709
But it doesn't count.
It's permanently stuck.

588
00:36:24,883 --> 00:36:27,114
That's as far as it'll open.

589
00:36:32,857 --> 00:36:36,555
I guess she could've jumped up,
caught the sill and pulled herself up.

590
00:36:36,728 --> 00:36:39,095
- No, not with her back, she couldn't.
- Mm.

591
00:36:39,264 --> 00:36:40,892
But maybe she could've...

592
00:36:41,066 --> 00:36:43,001
Maybe she could've
stood on this.

593
00:36:43,168 --> 00:36:45,160
That's where I keep
my gardening tools.

594
00:36:45,336 --> 00:36:47,896
Oh, no. How'd it
get broken like that?

595
00:36:49,908 --> 00:36:52,901
Ben, is that what I think it is?

596
00:36:57,549 --> 00:37:03,785
And so, what went wrong during
the operation, Miss Pierson?

597
00:37:03,955 --> 00:37:07,016
Dr. Gray accidentally
sliced open my right kidney

598
00:37:07,192 --> 00:37:09,320
and virtually destroyed it.

599
00:37:09,494 --> 00:37:12,760
And so, what did
you do as a result?

600
00:37:12,931 --> 00:37:15,025
- I sued for malpractice.
- Oh.

601
00:37:15,200 --> 00:37:17,066
And, uh, what were you awarded?

602
00:37:17,235 --> 00:37:18,931
One and a half million dollars.

603
00:37:20,572 --> 00:37:24,134
Boy, that's a lot of money
even when you say it fast.

604
00:37:24,309 --> 00:37:28,144
Especially when you see that
you're not exactly incapacitated.

605
00:37:28,313 --> 00:37:30,373
I'll be extremely incapacitated

606
00:37:30,548 --> 00:37:32,141
if anything happens
to my other kidney.

607
00:37:32,317 --> 00:37:35,378
Yeah, but the chances
are that nothing ever will,

608
00:37:35,553 --> 00:37:37,454
and that's why you
and Dr. Gray decided

609
00:37:37,622 --> 00:37:39,921
that this would be a
good gamble to take.

610
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:43,353
I don't know what
you're talking about.

611
00:37:43,528 --> 00:37:48,557
I'm saying that Dr. Bradley Gray
deliberately destroyed your kidney

612
00:37:48,733 --> 00:37:53,501
so that you and he could
become $1.5 million richer.

613
00:37:53,671 --> 00:37:55,071
Objection, Your Honor.

614
00:37:55,240 --> 00:37:58,233
Mr. Matlock has just given new
meaning to the word "speculation."

615
00:37:58,409 --> 00:38:00,503
Your Honor, it
would be speculation

616
00:38:00,678 --> 00:38:02,647
if I didn't have
proof, but I do.

617
00:38:02,814 --> 00:38:04,749
There's proof right here.

618
00:38:05,450 --> 00:38:07,419
- Overruled.
- Excellent.

619
00:38:07,585 --> 00:38:11,613
Uh, what's your phone
number, uh, Miss Pierson?

620
00:38:11,790 --> 00:38:13,520
Five-five-five-seven-eight-six-eight.

621
00:38:13,691 --> 00:38:14,852
Mm-hm.

622
00:38:15,026 --> 00:38:19,327
I had the, uh... I had
the phone company

623
00:38:19,497 --> 00:38:21,989
make a list of all the calls

624
00:38:22,167 --> 00:38:26,502
Dr. Gray dialed from
his house and his office

625
00:38:26,671 --> 00:38:31,837
in the five months prior to
his death in order of frequency.

626
00:38:32,010 --> 00:38:35,469
Would you read the
number at the top of the list?

627
00:38:35,647 --> 00:38:37,172
The one most often called.

628
00:38:37,348 --> 00:38:39,146
"Five-five-five-seven-eight-six-eight."

629
00:38:39,317 --> 00:38:41,117
- That's your number.
- Yes, but I told you...

630
00:38:41,219 --> 00:38:45,452
And this card was on his
desk at Shoreline Hospital.

631
00:38:45,623 --> 00:38:49,390
Would you tell the court
what that card represents?

632
00:38:49,561 --> 00:38:51,621
- Gentle Giant Movers.
- Mm-hm.

633
00:38:51,796 --> 00:38:55,426
Thank you. I wondered what
that was doing on his desk,

634
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:56,624
so I called them.

635
00:38:56,801 --> 00:38:58,463
And guess what.

636
00:38:58,636 --> 00:39:02,767
Dr. Gray had called
them too on March 6th,

637
00:39:02,941 --> 00:39:04,569
the day before he was murdered.

638
00:39:04,742 --> 00:39:08,577
And the man he spoke
to is Mr. C.J. Nussbaum.

639
00:39:08,746 --> 00:39:13,184
And he is prepared to testify
that Mr. Gray did not call

640
00:39:13,351 --> 00:39:15,081
because he was
thinking of moving.

641
00:39:15,253 --> 00:39:19,520
He called him to find
out if a friend of his was.

642
00:39:19,691 --> 00:39:22,251
A friend named Nancy Pierson.

643
00:39:24,095 --> 00:39:26,030
You were gonna pack
up bag and baggage

644
00:39:26,197 --> 00:39:27,756
and move to Dallas, weren't you?

645
00:39:27,932 --> 00:39:32,165
Only you were gonna keep it a
secret from your friend, Dr. Gray,

646
00:39:32,337 --> 00:39:33,361
because you had decided

647
00:39:33,538 --> 00:39:37,771
to keep all 1.5 million
yourself, hadn't you?

648
00:39:37,942 --> 00:39:39,774
He was not my friend.

649
00:39:39,944 --> 00:39:42,914
He called me all those
times to harass me.

650
00:39:43,081 --> 00:39:44,674
He must've called
the moving company

651
00:39:44,849 --> 00:39:47,842
so that he could continue to
harass me even after I left town.

652
00:39:52,357 --> 00:39:55,156
No. No.

653
00:39:55,326 --> 00:39:59,957
At some point... At some point,
you and he got, you know, close.

654
00:40:00,131 --> 00:40:02,100
And everybody
needs a little money.

655
00:40:02,267 --> 00:40:04,327
He needed money
because of his divorce,

656
00:40:04,502 --> 00:40:06,937
and so you and he
hatched up this scheme

657
00:40:07,105 --> 00:40:11,634
to defraud his insurance
company of a small fortune

658
00:40:11,809 --> 00:40:15,337
for sacrificing one
of your kidneys.

659
00:40:15,513 --> 00:40:18,039
But then you got to
thinking about that thing.

660
00:40:18,216 --> 00:40:22,153
"Wait a minute,
fair's fair here."

661
00:40:22,320 --> 00:40:25,085
And you decided to keep it all.

662
00:40:26,090 --> 00:40:28,389
But he found out.

663
00:40:30,028 --> 00:40:32,122
And he said,

664
00:40:33,131 --> 00:40:35,657
"Well, if I can't
have some of it,

665
00:40:36,534 --> 00:40:38,264
she's not gonna have it either."

666
00:40:38,836 --> 00:40:41,135
- No.
- And it almost worked.

667
00:40:41,306 --> 00:40:44,242
He put something that
you are deathly allergic to

668
00:40:44,409 --> 00:40:48,574
into one of those garlic
pills that you always take.

669
00:40:48,746 --> 00:40:50,977
He couldn't tell anybody
what you had done,

670
00:40:51,149 --> 00:40:53,311
because then they'd
know what he had done.

671
00:40:53,651 --> 00:40:55,142
You were double-crossing him,

672
00:40:55,320 --> 00:40:58,119
and he was helpless
to do anything about it.

673
00:40:58,289 --> 00:41:01,157
So he decided to kill you.

674
00:41:01,826 --> 00:41:05,388
He figured you wouldn't take
that garlic pill till you got to Dallas.

675
00:41:05,563 --> 00:41:10,194
And he had no idea, he
didn't think for a minute,

676
00:41:10,368 --> 00:41:12,200
that you would kill him first.

677
00:41:12,370 --> 00:41:13,702
Objection.

678
00:41:14,539 --> 00:41:16,872
Your Honor, I got
the proof right here.

679
00:41:17,875 --> 00:41:19,036
Proceed.

680
00:41:19,210 --> 00:41:20,701
Thank you.

681
00:41:24,716 --> 00:41:31,714
This box was found beside
the defendant's house.

682
00:41:33,992 --> 00:41:35,290
And there's a little window

683
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:41,229
that won't close all the way
anymore because the frame is warped.

684
00:41:41,399 --> 00:41:44,130
You stood on that box

685
00:41:44,869 --> 00:41:48,067
and dropped that scalpel
through that little opening

686
00:41:48,239 --> 00:41:51,334
into Rachel Stone's
house, didn't you?

687
00:41:51,509 --> 00:41:53,171
No, I did not.

688
00:41:53,344 --> 00:41:55,313
You've never been to
Rachel Stone's house?

689
00:41:55,480 --> 00:41:57,847
Of course not. I don't
even know where she lives.

690
00:41:58,016 --> 00:42:02,283
But since her name came
up during that malpractice suit,

691
00:42:02,453 --> 00:42:04,649
- you knew who she was, didn't you?
- Yes, but...

692
00:42:04,822 --> 00:42:06,085
And you knew

693
00:42:06,257 --> 00:42:09,352
that Dr. Gray had
caused her to be fired.

694
00:42:09,727 --> 00:42:12,356
And you knew she'd
be a perfect one to frame

695
00:42:12,530 --> 00:42:14,999
because everybody would
think that she killed him

696
00:42:15,166 --> 00:42:16,395
because he fired her.

697
00:42:16,567 --> 00:42:17,865
That's just not true.

698
00:42:18,036 --> 00:42:21,097
And so you eased
in that hospital,

699
00:42:21,272 --> 00:42:25,835
and you stole a pair
of gloves and a scalpel

700
00:42:26,444 --> 00:42:28,675
from the supply room.

701
00:42:28,880 --> 00:42:31,850
And then you killed Dr. Gray,

702
00:42:32,016 --> 00:42:35,384
then you looked up Rachel's
address and went over to her house,

703
00:42:35,553 --> 00:42:36,577
- didn't you?
- No.

704
00:42:36,754 --> 00:42:38,552
Walked around her house,

705
00:42:38,723 --> 00:42:41,955
walked around looking
for a way to get in.

706
00:42:42,527 --> 00:42:47,295
And then you saw...
You saw that little window.

707
00:42:47,465 --> 00:42:50,025
But it was 8 feet
above the ground.

708
00:42:50,201 --> 00:42:54,263
So you drug over this box,

709
00:42:54,439 --> 00:42:58,103
being very careful not
to hurt your back again.

710
00:42:58,276 --> 00:43:00,973
And you stood on it,

711
00:43:01,145 --> 00:43:04,946
and you dropped that bloody scalpel
through the window into her house.

712
00:43:05,550 --> 00:43:07,143
Then you went home,

713
00:43:07,552 --> 00:43:12,490
postponed your trip to Dallas
till the coast was absolutely clear.

714
00:43:12,657 --> 00:43:14,888
No, no. None...
None of this is true.

715
00:43:15,193 --> 00:43:16,786
Yes, it is.

716
00:43:16,961 --> 00:43:18,987
You know how I know?

717
00:43:24,502 --> 00:43:28,439
Because when you stepped
on this box to reach the window,

718
00:43:28,606 --> 00:43:31,337
you broke it, didn't
you? See that?

719
00:43:31,509 --> 00:43:33,102
Remember?

720
00:43:33,277 --> 00:43:36,247
See, your foot went all
the way through that slat.

721
00:43:36,414 --> 00:43:38,815
See there, you can see
your blood on the wood.

722
00:43:38,983 --> 00:43:40,178
See there?

723
00:43:40,351 --> 00:43:44,311
And the police found
good skin samples too.

724
00:43:44,489 --> 00:43:46,151
It wasn't me.

725
00:43:52,330 --> 00:43:53,593
Then prove it.

726
00:43:56,067 --> 00:43:58,969
- What do you mean?
- Let us see your legs.

727
00:43:59,137 --> 00:44:00,765
I don't mean all the way up.

728
00:44:00,938 --> 00:44:02,531
Just the calves.

729
00:44:02,707 --> 00:44:06,269
You're wearing slacks
anyhow. Just pull them up.

730
00:44:06,444 --> 00:44:09,278
If your legs have no
scratches on them,

731
00:44:09,447 --> 00:44:10,881
you're home free.

732
00:44:13,618 --> 00:44:16,645
I think that's a
